<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippe Pétain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Marshal Pétain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippe Pétain was a French general and marshal, celebrated as the defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> in 1916. In June 1940, as prime minister, he sought an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 10 July parliament gave him full powers; as head of the French State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> he pursued collaboration and an authoritarian National Revolution. Tried after the Liberation, he was sentenced to death for treason, commuted to life imprisonment, and died on the Île d'Yeu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippe-petain.jpg" alt="Philippe Pétain" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imprimerie Draeger à Montrouge en région parisienne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9tain_-_portrait_photographique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philippe Pétain was born on 24 April 1856 at Cauchy-à-la-Tour in the Pas-de-Calais, the only son of a peasant family. He entered the Saint-Cyr Military Academy in 1876 and served in various garrisons with the chasseurs, the elite light infantry, until 1899, after which he alternated between staff and regimental posts. By the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he held the rank of colonel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pétain led his brigade at the Battle of Guise in August 1914 and was promoted rapidly, commanding the Second Army from July 1915. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-verdun">Battle of Verdun</a> in 1916 he rotated divisions out after two weeks on the front line and organised truck transport along the Voie Sacrée, which helped to bring the German offensive to a halt in July. After the failure of General Nivelle's offensive in April 1917 provoked mutinies in nearly half the French infantry divisions on the Western Front, Pétain became Commander-in-Chief. He restored morale by talking to the men, promising no more suicidal attacks and granting rest and leave, and held 3,400 courts martial; 554 mutineers were sentenced to death, but over 90 per cent had their sentences commuted.</p><p>He received his baton as Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 8 December 1918. In the interwar years he was Inspector-General of the Army, commanded French forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> against the Rif tribes in 1925, was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> in 1931 and served as Minister of War in 1934. He became ambassador to Nationalist Spain in 1939 and joined Paul Reynaud's government as Deputy Prime Minister on 18 May 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 16 June 1940, as the Fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> neared, Reynaud resigned and President Albert Lebrun appointed Pétain in his place. His government sought an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 10 July the Congress of the French Parliament voted 569 to 80, with 18 abstentions, to give the cabinet power to draw up a new constitution, ending the Third Republic; nearly all French historians and every postwar French government regard the vote as illegal. The next day Pétain took near-absolute powers as head of the French State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>. He met Hitler at Montoire on 24 October 1940, and neither he nor his deputies Laval, Flandin and Darlan resisted German requests for indirect aid. In August 1944 the Germans moved him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>.</p><p>After the war Pétain was tried for treason from 23 July to 15 August 1945 and sentenced to death; the sentence was commuted to life imprisonment because of his age and service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He died on 23 July 1951 at Port-Joinville on the Île d'Yeu, aged 95. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> said his life was successively banal, then glorious, then deplorable, but never mediocre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippe Pétain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Admiral Jean Decoux governed for Vichy France from 1940 to 1945, promoting youth and sports movements and local patriotism loyal to <b>Marshal Pétain</b> while sharing the colony with Japanese forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Maréchal, nous voilà !&quot; (&quot;Marshal, here we are!&quot;) is a French propaganda song of 1941 in honour of Marshal <b>Philippe Pétain</b>, head of the Vichy regime, an authoritarian and antisemitic state set up during the German occupation.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 July 1940 parliament voted full powers to <b>Marshal Pétain</b>, ending the Republic and creating Vichy France.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 July the parliament meeting at Vichy voted full powers to Marshal <b>Philippe Pétain</b>, ending the French Third Republic and creating the French State.</blockquote><a href="/state/vichy-france">Vichy France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cauchy-à-la-Tour, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Port-Joinville. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.5,2.45&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Cauchy-à-la-Tour on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4606 410 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philippe Pétain"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5056 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5056 680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.5,2.45&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
